;
DISKS AND NAMED DIRECTORIES

A - The disk layout
B - Adding and mounting volumes
C - Naming user areas
:A
THE DISK LAYOUT

A0:    SYSTEM    Public commands, boot files and welcome text
A15:             Private board executable and static files
I0:    WORK      Temporary caller workspace
I13:   SYSUTLS   Private maintenance programs
I14:   HELP      Online documentation
I15:   RBBS      Private accounts and message data
J:               Swappable nominal 4 MB collections
P:               Swappable 512 MiB archives

P0:SIGM and P1:CPMUG are the default P: archive directories.
WORK is cleared at call boundaries. Do not keep sysop files there.
:B
ADDING AND MOUNTING VOLUMES

Place compatible J: images in disks/library/ and P: images in
disks/library/archives/. Use 1-8 letters, digits or underscores
for a catalog name, followed by .dsk.

J: z80pack-hd   4177920 bytes
P: z80pack-hdb  536870912 bytes

MOUNT /L lists the catalogs; MOUNT NAME selects the correct drive.
Use an explicit drive if names collide. MOUNT P: /DEFAULT restores
the default large archive. MOUNT J: /DEFAULT restores the small one.

Never edit a mounted image with host utilities. See DISK-IMAGES.DOC
for image creation. Keep private images outside the public catalogs.
Permissions follow the mounted DU, not the image's filename.
:C
NAMING USER AREAS

Create an extensionless marker such as -INFOCOM in the relevant DU.
Names have up to seven letters, digits or underscores.
NDSCAN J: rebuilds names for J: from its markers; MOUNT normally
does this automatically after a swap.

Duplicate names or invalid markers cause a scan failure. A successful
mount followed by a scan failure leaves the new image mounted with
the old names. Use explicit DU addresses until corrected.

A directory name does not grant access. AUTH determines permissions.
